  // the ball's travel band\n/* Anchor band (the printed scale ticks) used for vertical registration. It\n   OVERLAPS the ball band by 8 px, and that is measured, not sloppiness. The\n   reference is a median with no ball in it, so a ball reaching into this band\n   is a mismatch that MOVES with the flow — the obvious worry, and the right\n   one to have. Moving the band left to clear the ball makes every number\n   worse, because it slides off the ticks and onto the tube wall and the LED\n   glare, and the ticks ARE the registration signal. Measured 2026-08-22 over\n   the sweep, anchor left edge 217 -> 237: registration 0.908, 0.916, 0.951,\n   0.974, 0.982, 0.979 and ambiguity 7.1x, 9.6x, 11.4x, 13.5x, 14.8x, 16.3x.\n   Even the dy-versus-flow drift the overlap ought to cause is SMALLEST with\n   the overlap (0.09 px per 1000 rows at 233, against 0.46 with none). */\nconst AX1=233, AX2=261;\n/* Peak search range, in REFERENCE rows — dy is already removed when the\n   difference profile is built, so this window does not move with the camera.\n\n   It is the ball's PHYSICAL range plus clearance, not the picture's. Anything\n   outside is by construction not the ball, and letting it compete for the peak\n   is how the engine lost a whole afternoon twice: from 2026-08-16 13:09 and\n   2026-08-17 12:51 UTC low sun put a bright edge just below the old YTOP=60\n   and its clipped tail beat the real ball, while below the tube the chrome\n   nut's specular highlight was the resting ball's nearest rival all along.\n\n   Re-measured 2026-08-22 for the new camera pose and the LED. The ball now\n   lives at y 147 (above the printed scale) .. 447 (its resting stop), and both\n   rivals are still there:\n     YBOT 462..470 is a plateau at 14.8x ambiguity; 474 drops to 10.9x.\n     YTOP 95..115 sits at 14.8x and IMPROVES upward — 125 gives 15.9x, 135\n       gives 18.6x — but 135 starts pulling the over-scale reading, so the\n       usable range ends at 130.\n   The values below